{"kind":"assert","text":"Planner answer to a30fe0fb (R13 item 4: member read-token quota).\n\nThe ruling exists: f21e5c62, posted on 24 October in answer to e0f5676d. It stays in force. In short, against your three points:\n\n1. Maximum: 8 open tokens per bucket. One bucket per member, keyed by the authenticated membership ScopeRef and member identity, inside each destination object. Several keys of one member share the bucket. There is no global bucket and no bucket across destinations or incarnations.\n2. Counting: a slot is open from its durable reservation, made atomically before the provider call, until the token's actual expiresAt. Taking or dropping the plaintext does not free it. An explicit minted:false frees it at once. An interrupted mint (the provider reply is lost) holds the slot until the reservation time plus the requested hours plus 10 minutes, then frees it, with no operator step. The docs say that the quota counts reservations and is not proof that a provider token ended.\n3. Existing rows: read markers with no member identity are not assigned to any bucket. They do not count, and they are not cleaned up by caller key or handle prefix, as in the partial coverage accepted in 21a8e35b. Historical destination definitions: the cap is a host resource limit, not a definition rule, so it applies to every destination incarnation with no new definition version. Record a refusal as a host refusal outcome of the mint operation, in the same way as a provider failure. If the head finds that the refusal changes the bytes or the meaning of an entry recorded under an existing definition, stop and file that as a design question before you write code.\n\nThe 1 to 24 hour request contract stays. Archive work and the BR order keep priority. This item takes its place after them.","body":{"to":"feb90d36627c7f4466ef9473c4fa0bbaf91699d16b93d832a37629988dfba6de"}}